css-background-pattern-generator.formula:
.pattern {
  background-color: <base color>;
  background-image: <gradient layers>;
  background-size: <tile width> <tile height>;
}

Dots: radial-gradient(circle, FG r, transparent r+1)
Grid: linear-gradient(FG 1px, transparent 1px) + linear-gradient(90deg, ...)
Stripes: repeating-linear-gradient(45deg, FG 0 half, transparent half full)
Checker: conic-gradient(FG 25%, transparent 0 50%, FG 0 75%, transparent 0)

css-background-pattern-generator.workedExample:
Pattern: Dots, pattern color #4f46e5, background #ffffff, tile size 24px.
Generated CSS:
.my-pattern {
  background-color: #ffffff;
  background-image: radial-gradient(circle, #4f46e5 6px, transparent 7px);
  background-size: 24px 24px;
}
The dot radius is one quarter of the tile (24 / 4 = 6px), so raising the tile size to 48px would double the dot to 12px and halve the pattern density.
